APS-005 — Policy Evaluation

Document ID: APS-005
Title: Policy Evaluation
Version: 1.0.0-draft
Status: Draft

1. Purpose

This specification defines the Canonical Policy Evaluation Model (CPEM) for the Atlas Engineering Cognition Framework (AECF).

The Canonical Policy Evaluation Model establishes the normative process for interpreting, evaluating, and producing decisions from Canonical Policy Objects while preserving determinism, explainability, traceability, governance, and semantic consistency.

2. Scope

This specification defines:

Evaluation architecture
Evaluation lifecycle
Context resolution
Policy selection
Condition evaluation
Conflict detection
Decision production
Explainability

This specification applies to every policy evaluation performed within Atlas.

3. Canonical Definition

Policy Evaluation is the governed process of interpreting one or more Canonical Policy Objects against a defined evaluation context to produce a deterministic policy decision.

Evaluation determines policy outcomes.

It SHALL NOT modify policy definitions.

4. Objectives

The Policy Evaluation Model SHALL:

Produce deterministic decisions.
Preserve policy semantics.
Support explainability.
Support traceability.
Support governance.
Support interoperability.
Support reproducibility.
Enable policy composition.
5. Evaluation Principles

Every evaluation SHALL satisfy the following principles.

Deterministic

Equivalent policy sets and equivalent contexts SHALL produce equivalent decisions.

Side-Effect Free

Evaluation SHALL NOT modify:

Policies
Context
Canonical Atlas Objects

Evaluation is a read-only process.

Explainable

Every decision SHALL identify:

Policies evaluated
Conditions evaluated
Applicable rules
Final reasoning
Traceable

Every evaluation SHALL preserve:

Context
Policy versions
Inputs
Outputs
Evidence
Governed

Evaluation SHALL follow governance policies.

Version Aware

Only explicitly selected policy versions SHALL participate in evaluation.

6. Evaluation Inputs

Every evaluation SHALL define:

Input	Description
Evaluation Request	Invocation
Evaluation Context	Operational context
Applicable Policies	Candidate policy set
Canonical Objects	Objects under evaluation
Supporting Evidence	Evidence references
Trust Context	Security assertions
Applicable Profiles	Governance profiles

Inputs SHALL remain immutable throughout evaluation.

7. Evaluation Lifecycle

Every policy evaluation SHALL progress through the following lifecycle.

Requested
     │
Prepared
     │
Context Resolved
     │
Policy Selected
     │
Conditions Evaluated
     │
Conflicts Resolved
     │
Decision Produced
     │
Obligations Generated
     │
Recorded

Each stage SHALL be auditable.

8. Context Resolution

Evaluation SHALL resolve all required context before evaluating policies.

Context MAY include:

Object metadata
Lifecycle state
Trust assertions
Relationships
Workflow state
Time
Organization
Project
Regulatory jurisdiction

Missing mandatory context SHALL result in an incomplete evaluation.

9. Policy Selection

Atlas SHALL identify the applicable policies using:

Policy scope
Object applicability
Organization
Project
Lifecycle state
Security classification
Compliance profile
Policy version

Only selected policies SHALL participate in evaluation.

10. Condition Evaluation

Each selected policy SHALL evaluate:

Preconditions
Conditions
Constraints
Dependencies
Required relationships
Required evidence

Conditions SHALL NOT modify evaluation context.

11. Conflict Detection

Evaluation SHALL detect policy conflicts.

Examples include:

Conflict	Example
Permit vs Deny	Same action
Multiple obligations	Different deadlines
Competing priorities	Equal precedence
Scope overlap	Conflicting applicability
Version conflict	Different active versions

Conflicts SHALL be explicitly identified.

12. Conflict Resolution

Atlas SHALL resolve conflicts using:

Explicit override relationships.
Higher governance authority.
Higher policy priority.
More specific applicability.
Newer approved policy version.
Human escalation if unresolved.

Silent conflict resolution SHALL NOT occur.

13. Decision Production

Evaluation SHALL produce one or more policy decisions.

Canonical decisions include:

Decision	Meaning
Permit	Authorized
Deny	Prohibited
Require	Mandatory
Recommend	Advisory
Warn	Warning
Escalate	Human decision required

Decisions SHALL reference the responsible policies.

14. Obligation Generation

Policy decisions MAY generate obligations.

Each obligation SHALL define:

Responsible participant
Required action
Completion criteria
Due date
Escalation policy

Obligations SHALL be governed Canonical Atlas Objects.

15. Explainability

Every evaluation SHALL preserve:

Applicable policies
Evaluation sequence
Conditions satisfied
Conditions failed
Conflicts
Resolution process
Final decision

Explainability SHALL be sufficient for independent review.

16. Evaluation Record

Every evaluation SHALL generate an immutable evaluation record containing:

Evaluation identifier
Evaluation timestamp
Evaluated policy versions
Context snapshot
Produced decisions
Generated obligations
Evidence references
Audit metadata

Evaluation records SHALL conform to the Canonical Atlas Object model.

17. Conformance

A policy evaluation implementation conforms if it:

Implements the canonical evaluation lifecycle.
Preserves deterministic evaluation.
Resolves context before evaluation.
Detects policy conflicts.
Produces explainable decisions.
Generates immutable evaluation records.
Maintains complete traceability.
18. Future Evolution

Future revisions MAY introduce:

Parallel policy evaluation.
Distributed policy evaluation.
Incremental evaluation.
Predictive policy evaluation.
AI-assisted explanation generation.
Federated evaluation services.

Extensions SHALL preserve canonical evaluation semantics.

